2. PAINT SYSTEM (web implementation)
==================================================
Design and build a body-painting tool:
- A paint panel opens with a key (F) and a mirror/preview of the character so the player can
  see their body from all sides. Rotate the preview with the mouse.
- Tools: brush (size and softness), eraser, fill bucket, undo/redo (20 steps), symmetry toggle
  (left/right mirror), full color picker plus a recent-colors strip.
- EYEDROPPER: pick a color directly from the 3D world by sampling the rendered scene at the
  crosshair (render-target pixel read). This is the most important tool; make it fast.
- "Hold-up" helper: a toggle that shows the nearby surface behind the player as a faint overlay
  so they can match patterns (pattern-matching assist; limited use or disabled in ranked).
- Body regions: UV-map the character so every part (head, torso, arms, hands, legs, feet) has
  its own paint area. Highlight unpainted white areas with a warning indicator and show
  "paint completeness %" so beginners learn the white-elbows lesson.
- Technical: each hider has a 512x512 canvas texture (256x256 on low quality). Paint locally
  with no network traffic. When prep ends, compress the texture (PNG or WebP, under ~60 KB),
  upload it ONCE to the server, and the server relays it to others. During prep, other
  players see only a white or neutral body, so nobody can copy a painting live.
- Server validation: max size, rate limits, image format checks, and an automated content check
  plus a report button for offensive drawings (paint abuse is a real moderation risk). Store a
  hash and the texture so moderators can review reports.
- Lighting matters: use lit materials with baked lighting and soft shadows so paint reacts to
  light like the environment. Seekers must not get a shader advantage; consider subtle film
  grain on the seeker view so pixel-scanning cheats are harder.
- Mobile later: touch painting with a simplified tool set.

