3. MAPS (design 5 original maps; build 1 first)
==================================================
Map design rules (apply to every map):
- Every map needs PAINT-FRIENDLY richness: a mix of patterns (tiles, wallpaper, wood grain,
  fabric, graffiti, foliage, mosaics), varied lighting (bright, dim, colored light, shadows),
  and surfaces of different scales so hiders can blend at many levels of difficulty.
- Avoid large flat single-color walls everywhere (too easy) and avoid pure visual noise
  everywhere (too frustrating). Balance both.
- Size for 20 players: roughly 120 to 180 m across, with multiple floors/zones, at least
  25 distinct hide zones, and loops so seekers cannot cover everything from one spot.
- Include "hero spots": two or three iconic hiding places per map people will clip and share.
- Spawn: sealed seeker room; hiders spread across 6 or more spawn points.
- Keep scenes light: instanced props, baked lighting, LOD, under ~25 MB per map.
- Provide a map metadata file (name, size, recommended players, spawn points, hide-zone
  tags) and server collision data.

Original map concepts (Nigeria-inspired and fun to clip):
1. "Open-Air Market Street": stalls, fabric rolls, fruit crates, tarpaulins, painted signs,
   narrow alleys, generator corner. Strong color and pattern variety.
2. "Wedding Hall": ankara/aso-ebi patterned tablecloths, decor walls, stage, buffet area,
   colored party lights. Great for bold patterned painting and lighting changes.
3. "Danfo Bus Depot": buses with graffiti and stripes, tires, oil drums, workshop, rusty
   metal textures. Good for gritty, high-contrast hiding.
4. "Old School Compound": classrooms, chalkboards, corridors, field with trees, murals. Mix of
   indoor and outdoor, beginner-friendly.
5. "Beach Resort": pool area, cabanas, palms, striped umbrellas, rooftop bar. Bright open
   light, harder to blend, high-risk and high-reward.
Also design a MAP EDITOR concept (later phase): glTF import plus zone tagging, so the community
can submit maps, because community maps extend a hide-and-seek game's life.

